## Before class

**Read both of these before you come — we'll build on them right away:**

1. **[Creative Problem Solving handbook](https://brdo.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/cps_handbook.pdf)** — a 33-page handbook; you are assigned a focused excerpt, not the whole thing:
    - **Read closely:** **pp. 1 and 3** (what CPS is, and the one-page summary of the principles) and **pp. 6–10** — the divergent/convergent "dynamic balance," both sets of principles, deferring judgment, and the four-stage overview. This is the heart of it and the part class builds on.
    - **Read the tool write-ups we actually use in class:** *Posing Problems as Questions* and *Webbing / Ladder of Abstraction* (**pp. 17–18**), and *Post-It Brainstorming* and *Brainwriting* (**p. 19**).
    - **Skim:** pp. 11–16, the per-stage detail.
    - **Skip:** pp. 4–5 (facilitation roles) and pp. 29–33 (about the publisher). The handbook is written for facilitated group sessions, and those pages are the parts that do not apply to us.
2. **["Channeling Your Creativity,"](https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/new-era/2004/02/channeling-your-creativity?lang=eng)** Elder Robert D. Hales (*New Era*, Feb 2004).

**Also bring:** your <course-link type="page" id="pg-idea-journal">idea journal</course-link> — with a week's worth of entries — and a handful of raw problems you've noticed.

---

## Purpose

This is our first real workshop, and it's mostly *doing.* You'll practice the Creative Problem Solving process hands-on with a quick warm-up exercise, then turn it loose on the real task: **generating a big, wide pool of problems and needs worth founding a project on.** You'll defer judgment, go for quantity, and pour it all into your idea journal — this is the raw material for your <course-link type="assignment" id="cp1">Idea Briefs</course-link>.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this session, you'll be able to:

1. **Describe the four stages of Creative Problem Solving** and apply divergent, then convergent, thinking to a problem.
2. **Practice deferring judgment** — generate many ideas before evaluating any of them.
3. **Use several divergent tools** (Post-It brainstorming, brainwriting, and the ladder of abstraction) to explore a problem space.
4. **Generate a broad set of candidate problems** framed around real people and their needs — *not* solutions.
5. **Leave with a much bigger idea journal** and a shortlist of problem spaces to investigate.

---

## What we'll do together

A quick recap and journal check-in; a short consolidation of the CPS process from the reading; a discussion of the Hales article; a hands-on warm-up exercise; and then the main event — a fast, wide problem-finding workshop using the tools above, capturing everything into your journals.

---

## What you'll leave with

- **A materially expanded idea journal** — a lot more candidate problems than you walked in with.
- **3–5 "starred" problem spaces** that tug at you, to investigate further (not a commitment — just a shortlist).
- **Before next class — <course-link type="page" id="s03">{{ S03_DAY }}, {{ S03_DATE }}</course-link> (Week 2):** keep feeding your journal and bring your starred problems. Week 2 turns to open source, licensing, and **discovery** — how to talk to real users and understand their needs.

